THE ACCUMULATION FAILURE, and it is a new one worth naming.

'Rather than having each letter emit a glow of its own' revealed that I had
THREE glyph-shaped light sources stacked on the hero name — canvas shadowBlur
per dot, a drop-shadow on the canvas, and a radial ellipse. I added them across
three separate passes, each time answering a 'make it brighter' note by adding
a layer rather than by fixing the one already there. Nobody asked for three;
each was locally reasonable and the set was absurd.

The tell was available every pass and I never read it: I was writing comments
that said 'THREE layers of glow, each doing a different job'. Enumerating your
own accumulated workarounds in a comment is a smell, not documentation. When a
comment has to justify why N mechanisms coexist, the honest move is to delete
N-1.

Deleting all three for one rectangle also took the home page from 28 to 60 fps
(/more 55 -> 60), because canvas shadowBlur forces a blurred rasterisation of
the entire draw pass every frame. The visual fix and the perf fix were the same
edit. Layers you added defensively are usually not free.

SECOND, ON SCOPE OF A DIRECTIVE. Last pass's 'make everything in experience
white/silver' — I applied it to the tech badges too, and the correction came
back as 'less stale...the stack can be alternate colors'. The rule the user
actually held was about CHROME (halo, border, accent bar, title, divider), not
CONTENT. Badges are content, where variation is information rather than noise.
When a styling directive says 'everything', check whether it means the frame or
the things inside it before flattening both.

Also: the intermittent orange line on the country cards was a hover panel parked
at exactly translateY(100%) — its edge landing exactly on a rounded, clipped,
fractionally-positioned grid cell's edge. Sub-pixel rounding decided whether it
showed, which is why it was 'occasional'. Fixed with 101% AND visibility:hidden;
the second is the actual guarantee, the first just moves the edge off the
boundary. Verified at 7 widths including deliberately awkward ones.

